WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.333 --> 00:00:02.566
Timothy: You know, Drea,
I've been really looking forward

00:00:02.566 --> 00:00:05.133
to all the machine learning
at Cloud Next this year.

00:00:05.133 --> 00:00:06.366
Alexandrea:
That's a hot topic here.

00:00:06.366 --> 00:00:07.533
Timothy: It is a hot topic.

00:00:07.533 --> 00:00:09.666
So it's about time
that we checked it out.

00:00:09.666 --> 00:00:10.800
We're here with Kaz.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.666
Kaz, would you mind
introducing yourself

00:00:12.666 --> 00:00:13.800
and telling everybody out there
what you do?

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.800
Kaz: Thank you so much.
I'm Kaz.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:17.566
I'm a Developer Advocate
for Google Cloud.

00:00:17.566 --> 00:00:21.266
Here we have so-called "Find
Your Candy" demonstrations

00:00:21.266 --> 00:00:24.400
where you can tell
some voice command to the tablet

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:27.166
so that the robot can understand
your voice command

00:00:27.166 --> 00:00:29.466
and try to find the best candy
for you

00:00:29.466 --> 00:00:32.166
and pick it up
by recognizing the image.

00:00:32.166 --> 00:00:33.966
Timothy: So there's a number

00:00:33.966 --> 00:00:36.133
of different machine learning
algorithms going on there,

00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:38.133
both in the voice recognition
and understanding

00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:40.866
no just what you said,
but what it means,

00:00:40.866 --> 00:00:42.166
as well as selecting the candy.

00:00:42.166 --> 00:00:43.866
Can you just walk us through
that flow?

00:00:43.866 --> 00:00:46.666
Kaz: Yeah, so initially,
it's using the voice--

00:00:46.666 --> 00:00:48.966
Speech API to recognize
your voice,

00:00:48.966 --> 00:00:51.133
and then it uses
the Natural Language API

00:00:51.133 --> 00:00:54.033
to understand the words
inside the sentence

00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:56.333
and then it uses
Cloud Machine Learning

00:00:56.333 --> 00:01:00.100
to use the algorithm--
it's called word2vec.

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:01.533
word2vec is an algorithm

00:01:01.533 --> 00:01:04.466
that's used to understand
the meaning of each word.

00:01:04.466 --> 00:01:06.266
So it understands the similarity

00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:09.733
between the hot and spicy
or creamy and milky.

00:01:09.733 --> 00:01:13.733
And it tries to use the word
to choose the best candy

00:01:13.733 --> 00:01:14.833
to pick up.

00:01:14.833 --> 00:01:16.933
And finally,
it uses the Cloud ML again

00:01:16.933 --> 00:01:18.600
to recognize the--

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:22.266
to the image recognition
on each object.

00:01:22.266 --> 00:01:23.500
Timothy: That's a lot of machine
learning.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:24.566
Alexandrea: Right.

00:01:24.566 --> 00:01:26.366
Kaz: Yes. A lot, yeah.

00:01:26.366 --> 00:01:27.633
Timothy: All right,
shall we try it?

00:01:27.633 --> 00:01:30.866
Kaz: Yeah, you can try it.

00:01:30.866 --> 00:01:31.866
Alexandrea: Let's see.

00:01:31.866 --> 00:01:34.333
I like mints.

00:01:34.333 --> 00:01:37.466
Little voice recognition.

00:01:37.466 --> 00:01:40.033
Timothy: I see and it's finding
the whole sentence there.

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:42.433
It knows what each of those
words is.

00:01:42.433 --> 00:01:44.700
I bet you it's gonna pick out
the noun, right?

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.133
Kaz: Yes.
So it understands all the--

00:01:47.133 --> 00:01:50.000
the part of speech, right,
noun, adjective.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.766
And now it's choosing
the best candy for you.

00:01:52.766 --> 00:01:54.633
Timothy: Hey, look,
it chooses mints.

00:01:54.633 --> 00:01:56.633
[laughter]

00:01:56.633 --> 00:01:58.666
Alexandrea: So talk--'cause I know
we're sorting some candy here,

00:01:58.666 --> 00:02:00.966
but talk to us, like, how this
translates for enterprises

00:02:00.966 --> 00:02:02.100
and for businesses.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.000
Kaz: Actually, this is
a miniature version

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:05.366
of the real world use cases,

00:02:05.366 --> 00:02:09.366
and here we have
all the displays

00:02:09.366 --> 00:02:11.166
for the cucumber sorter.

00:02:11.166 --> 00:02:14.300
Actually, we have a real
cucumber farmer in Japan

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.766
who introduce--who used
the same technologies

00:02:16.766 --> 00:02:19.033
to build his own
cucumber sorter.

00:02:19.033 --> 00:02:21.200
And he spend only $15

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:22.633
to build his own cucumber sorter

00:02:22.633 --> 00:02:26.066
by using Google Cloud machine
learning technology.

00:02:26.066 --> 00:02:27.933
Timothy: That's really cool.
Kaz: Yeah.

00:02:27.933 --> 00:02:30.566
Alexandrea: So, essentially, it
takes the best prototype of that

00:02:30.566 --> 00:02:32.100
and kind of buckets them, or--

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.100
Kaz: Yeah, yeah.

00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:35.833
So he wanted to

00:02:35.833 --> 00:02:39.166
build a machine that can sort
out all the cucumbers

00:02:39.166 --> 00:02:40.666
into nine different classes.

00:02:40.666 --> 00:02:43.933
he took nine thousand different
photos of these cucumbers

00:02:43.933 --> 00:02:47.233
and now and also built the
sorter by using Raspberry Pi

00:02:47.233 --> 00:02:49.266
and downloaded TensorFlow,

00:02:49.266 --> 00:02:51.066
the machine learning library
from Google,

00:02:51.066 --> 00:02:52.566
to build his own sorter.

00:02:52.566 --> 00:02:54.600
And the point here is that
you don't have to be

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.000
a cutting-edge scientist

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.033
or machine learning expert
to get started

00:02:59.033 --> 00:03:01.700
with the latest machine learning
technology.

